---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Navbar from '../components/Navbar.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

const releases = [
  {
    version: '3.4.0',
    date: 'July 29, 2026',
    title: 'What a full dogfood run taught us',
    summary: 'A complete <code>gspec build</code> against a real engine — a self-hosted read-later service, six features, two-tier specs, tiered models — and almost everything it found was something the test suite structurally could not: fixtures encode what the author <em>intended</em>, not what a model actually does.<br /><br /><strong>The good news first.</strong> The origin/delta discipline held on real specs: 83 anchors, 7 genuinely shared across features, <strong>zero duplicate origins</strong>. The feature written last found every prior definition across five sibling folders and wrote deltas stating only what changed — even correctly deciding two components needed no delta at all. The architecture altitude rules held too: zero entity field lists, zero endpoint inventories, zero of the detail v3.1 was built to evict.<br /><br /><strong>The rest is what broke.</strong> Half of v3.2 §7 was never implemented. The style writer picked the format the design system depends on <em>not</em> having. Six of six feature architectures blew a budget that was simply set wrong. And a transient engine failure reported nothing but <code>exited 1</code>, costing three manual reproductions to establish it was transient.',
    changes: [
      {
        type: 'added',
        description: '<strong><code>arch.md</code> is structure; <code>design.html</code> is appearance.</strong> The designer was fenced out of behavior from the start — "visual, not behavioral" — but the architect was never fenced out of <em>appearance</em>, and it filled the gap: <strong>255 style-token references across six <code>## UI</code> sections</strong>, plus pixel measurements, hover styling and layout dimensions. A mockup written in prose, sitting beside a mockup that renders. The reciprocal rule now exists on both sides, with one test — <em>could a designer change this without changing any behavior?</em> — and the validator raises it as a major finding. Components, props, state ownership, data flow and keyboard behavior stay exactly where they were.',
      },
      {
        type: 'fixed',
        description: '<strong>The style guide\'s tokens-only budgets were never applied.</strong> v3.2 rescoped the style guide to tokens and moved component styling to each feature\'s design — but only the persona changed; the budgets stayed at their pre-v3 values. So a 2,796-word guide reported as 1.4× over when against the intended ceiling it was 3.1×, and the measurement meant to detect "the split did not happen" was itself unchanged. Now 900 words (Markdown) and 700 prose (HTML), as specified.',
      },
      {
        type: 'fixed',
        description: '<strong>The autonomous build never told the style writer which format to write.</strong> Its contract says the brief decides and to treat the brief as authoritative — but an autonomous run has no interview to settle it, so the choice fell to model preference and it picked Markdown. That is no longer neutral: each feature\'s <code>design.html</code> copies the guide\'s CSS custom-property block verbatim, and a Markdown guide has none, dropping the token check from an exact comparison to a best-effort transcription. The style stage now states the preference where the writer is actually looking.',
      },
      {
        type: 'changed',
        description: '<strong>The feature architecture budget was miscalibrated.</strong> Six of six features exceeded 2,400 words (1.1×–1.8×). That is not six writers failing — it is the wrong ceiling for the one file whose entire job is restating the stack and architecture so nothing downstream has to, and a ceiling every honest draft breaks teaches writers to ignore ceilings. Raised to 3,000, above the observed median and still under the outlier — which was mostly the prose mockup the appearance boundary now removes.',
      },
      {
        type: 'fixed',
        description: '<strong>A failed writer now says what the engine said.</strong> <code>exited 1</code> alone cannot distinguish a transient overload (resume and it passes) from a broken prompt (resuming loops forever) — and that is exactly when a user is least equipped to dig. A run failed here twice and reported nothing else; establishing it was transient took three manual reproductions. The engine\'s own output is now carried into the failure, and a silent failure says so explicitly and notes that <code>--resume</code> retries just that item.',
      },
      {
        type: 'changed',
        description: '<strong><code>architecture-validator</code> moves to the strong tier, on a stated principle.</strong> A validator\'s tier should follow how much judgment is left <em>after</em> its lint. Most now sit behind a deterministic floor; this one has none, and the two things it alone polices — no feature detail crept in, no sections invented — are pure judgment. On the cheap tier it passed an architecture 1.7× its budget carrying five sections outside the required list. It also now polices that list as a <strong>ceiling</strong>, not just a floor.',
      },
      {
        type: 'changed',
        description: '<strong>A Modules row is a verification unit, not a code module.</strong> A brief saying "two modules, deployed separately" correctly produced a <em>one-row</em> table — one pnpm workspace, one build command — which reads like a bug to anyone who wrote that brief. The rename traded one confusion for another, so the architect now states this in the section\'s prose whenever the row count is lower than the number of components a reader would count.',
      },
      {
        type: 'fixed',
        description: '<strong>The QA one-liner reported model preamble instead of the finding.</strong> A validator prefaced its verdict with reasoning, and the run log showed <em>that</em> as the entire reason the stage failed — the actual blocker (a missing machine-readable enforcement block) never appeared. The summary now skips past the verdict line to the summary or first severity-tagged finding.',
      },
    ],
    migration: 'Run <code>npx gspec</code>. Existing specs are untouched. Your next <code>/gspec-architect</code> or feature-architecture QA pass will start flagging appearance detail in <code>## UI</code> and sections outside the architecture\'s required list — both were always contract, and are now actually checked.',
  },
  {
    version: '3.3.2',
    date: 'July 28, 2026',
    title: 'A scripted install no longer stops halfway and calls it success',
    summary: 'Found while setting up a dogfood run: <code>gspec install --models recommended</code> did nothing on a re-install. The cause was worse than the symptom. Every prompt shares one stdin, and off a TTY the fallback only settled when a line <em>arrived</em> — so the moment a script piped fewer answers than there are prompts, the promise hung, node drained its event loop, and the process <strong>exited 0 having skipped every remaining step</strong>.<br /><br />That is an install reporting success with no hooks installed, no harness preamble, and no model assignment — silently, and only in the non-interactive path that CI and setup scripts use. Running out of piped input now means "take the default", which is what a non-interactive caller means by staying quiet.',
    changes: [
      {
        type: 'fixed',
        description: '<strong>An install that runs out of piped answers completes instead of vanishing.</strong> The off-TTY prompt now settles in both exhaustion cases: stdin ending <em>while</em> a prompt waits, and — the common one — stdin that ended <em>before</em> the prompt, which is every prompt after the first once a script's input is used up. Regression-tested end to end: a fully unanswered install still reaches its last step, and a re-install with a single piped answer both completes and writes the model assignment.',
      },
    ],
    migration: 'Run <code>npx gspec</code>. If you install gspec from a script or CI step, re-run it: any prior scripted install may have stopped partway through without saying so — check that <code>.claude/hooks/</code> (or your target's equivalent) is populated.',
  },
  {
    version: '3.3.1',
    date: 'July 28, 2026',
    title: 'A migrated plan is never regenerated over',
    summary: 'Found by running the v3 pipeline against this site\'s own migrated specs rather than against fixtures. The per-feature stages only skipped regeneration when the manifest showed <em>they</em> had written the file — so a <code>tasks.md</code> that arrived through <code>/gspec-migrate</code>, or was written by hand, had no such record and was regenerated over on the next build. The old plan stage skipped on mere existence for exactly this reason; its bug was that it also skipped <em>validation</em>, and in fixing that the preservation went with it.<br /><br />An existing, well-formed file is now never regenerated — whoever wrote it — and is still validated either way. Regeneration happens only when the file is missing or malformed.',
    changes: [
      {
        type: 'fixed',
        description: '<strong>A hand-authored or migrated feature file is no longer overwritten.</strong> <code>arch.md</code>, <code>design.html</code>, and <code>tasks.md</code> are regenerated only when missing or malformed; a file already present is kept and validated, and the log says whether this run wrote it. For <code>tasks.md</code> this matters most: regenerating over it would discard the checked-task history the immutability contract exists to protect, and relying on the writer to preserve it put a data-loss risk behind agent discipline rather than in front of it.',
      },
      {
        type: 'fixed',
        description: '<strong><code>plan-decomposer</code>\'s input contract was stale.</strong> It still described the existing plan as living at <code>gspec/tasks/&lt;slug&gt;.md</code> and being handed to it — wrong on both counts after the feature-folder move. It now names the folder path and says plainly to read any plan already there, reproducing every checked task verbatim and appending new work with the next free ID.',
      },
    ],
    migration: 'Run <code>npx gspec</code>. Nothing changes on disk. If you migrated a project on 3.2.x and have not re-run a build since, this is worth taking first — a build on 3.2.x would have regenerated your migrated plans rather than preserving them.',
  },
  {
    version: '3.3.0',
    date: 'July 28, 2026',
    title: 'The cheapest thing in the build stops running on the most expensive model',
    summary: 'gspec has supported per-agent models since 2.6.0, and almost nobody sets one — so every agent runs on the engine\'s default, which is usually the top tier. That includes the <strong>validators</strong>, which are roughly half of all agent runs in a v3 build and the cheapest thing to move: the deterministic lints added in 3.2 and 3.2.1 now handle the mechanical checks, so what is left for a validator is focused judgment over one small file.<br /><br />The tiering was already written down — it just lived in the README as prose, where it did nothing. It now ships as a table the installer can write for you: <strong>validators cheap, everyday authoring balanced, and the three load-bearing jobs strong</strong> (the system design, each feature\'s own architecture, and the code, where a mistake propagates into everything downstream).<br /><br />It is an <em>offer</em>, not a default. Which models a run bills to is the user\'s call, so the install asks, never overwrites a <code>models</code> map you already have, and writes nothing at all in a non-interactive run unless you pass the flag.',
    changes: [
      {
        type: 'added',
        description: '<strong>A recommended model assignment, offered at install.</strong> On a Claude or Codex install gspec shows the tiering and writes it to <code>.gspec/config.json</code> if you accept. <code>gspec install -t claude --models recommended</code> takes it without being asked; <code>--models none</code> skips the question. Three guards: an existing <code>models</code> map (project <em>or</em> global) is never touched, a non-TTY install writes nothing without the flag, and only the two engines that actually run the build\'s agents are covered — cursor and opencode do not, and pi\'s roster moves too fast to ship in a table.',
      },
      {
        type: 'fixed',
        description: '<strong>Two agents could not be reached by a role tier at all.</strong> The <code>writer</code> selector keys on the <code>-writer</code> suffix, so <code>feature-architect</code> and <code>feature-designer</code> — added in 3.2.0 — matched only <code>default</code>. Anyone tiering by role would have silently missed them, including the recommendation itself. Both now map to the <code>writer</code> tier, with <code>feature-architect</code> overridden by name to the strong model, since a feature\'s architecture is what every one of its tasks is built from.',
      },
      {
        type: 'changed',
        description: '<strong>The README\'s recommendation and the shipped table are the same thing.</strong> The documented example was the only place these model IDs lived, and it had drifted to a superseded Opus. There is now one table in <code>lib/config.js</code>, the docs describe what the installer actually writes, and keeping up with a new model is a one-line edit in one place.',
      },
    ],
    migration: 'Run <code>npx gspec</code> and answer the model question, or <code>gspec install -t claude --models recommended</code> to take the assignment directly. Nothing changes for existing projects unless you opt in — and if you already have a <code>models</code> map, gspec leaves it exactly as it is.',
  },
  {
    version: '3.2.1',
    date: 'July 28, 2026',
    title: 'A checker the implementer did not write, and an audit that stops growing',
    summary: 'Two gaps the feature-folder release left open.<br /><br /><strong>The implement gate was producer-equals-checker.</strong> <code>verify.sh</code> is deterministic, but the implementer writes both the code and the tests it runs — so a green run says nothing about a checked task whose file was never created, a stub sitting behind a checked box, or a capability ticked while its covering tasks are still open. All three fell to one broad agentic judgment, which is expensive and vague. They are pure text checks, so they now run <em>before</em> the validator: findings go straight back with the exact problem at zero agent cost, and <code>implementation-validator</code> narrows to what only judgment can answer.<br /><br /><strong>The audit had become the new unbounded reader.</strong> v3 moved the "reads everything, grows with age" problem out of the implementer — and into <code>codebase-inspector</code>, which read every feature folder, and folders accumulate for the life of a project. It is now incremental against a recorded commit. That also makes room for the drift category this layout could not otherwise catch: enriched folders inline the ORM and the framework on purpose, and <strong>nothing re-checked them when the stack changed</strong>.',
    changes: [
      {
        type: 'added',
        description: '<strong>An implementation lint runs before the judgment gate.</strong> A checked task naming a file that does not exist; an unfinished-work marker in a file a checked task claimed to complete; a PRD capability checked while a task covering it is still open. Each is a deterministic finding naming exactly what is wrong, fixed without spending a validator run — where a broad "the implementation seems incomplete" FAIL would send a self-heal run off to rediscover it. Scoped strictly to what <em>checked</em> tasks claim: unfinished work is not a finding, it is just unfinished.',
      },
      {
        type: 'changed',
        description: '<strong><code>implementation-validator</code> narrows to judgment.</strong> It no longer re-derives what the lint just checked, and it validates <strong>per feature folder against its code</strong> rather than judging the repository as a whole — which is where agentic code review gets vague. Its budget goes to whether the acceptance criteria are actually met, whether the tests meaningfully assert them, and whether the UI honors the feature\'s <code>design.html</code>.',
      },
      {
        type: 'added',
        description: '<strong>The audit is incremental.</strong> It records the commit it last ran at in <code>.gspec/audit-baseline</code> and inspects only the feature folders and source files that changed since, plus the always-small root-tier specs. Every case it cannot answer cheaply and correctly — no baseline, not a git repo, an unreadable ref, a root-tier spec that changed — falls back to a <em>full</em> sweep, because a silently narrow audit is worse than a slow one.',
      },
      {
        type: 'added',
        description: '<strong>New drift category: <code>enrichment</code>.</strong> This closes the one guarantee v3 traded away against v2\'s single-source-of-truth. Feature folders inline the stack and style decisions so an implementer needs nothing else — which means nothing re-checks them when those decisions change, and a folder can go on naming a library the project replaced months ago. The audit now compares each in-scope folder\'s inlined claims against the current stack, style, and code. Checking it only over the changed set is what lets the correctness fix land without reintroducing the cost problem.',
      },
    ],
    migration: 'Run <code>npx gspec</code> to install the updated agents and floors. Nothing changes on disk in your project. The first audit after upgrading is a full sweep (there is no baseline yet) and writes one afterwards; subsequent audits are incremental. Expect the implement gate to be stricter than before — it will now stop for a checked task whose file is missing or a capability checked ahead of its plan, both of which previously passed silently.',
  },
  {
    version: '3.2.0',
    date: 'July 28, 2026',
    title: 'Everything about a feature lives in one folder',
    summary: 'A plan used to be a bare task list. Every agent that read one had to also load the profile, the stack, the style guide, the architecture, and the PRD to have enough context to act — and the implement stage is where that cost multiplies, because each scope spawns up to six fresh agents and every one of them re-reads from scratch.<br /><br />A feature is now a <strong>folder</strong>: <code>gspec/features/&lt;slug&gt;/</code> holding <code>prd.md</code> beside <code>arch.md</code> (that feature\'s own data, API, UI and logic), <code>design.html</code> (a mockup you can actually open), and <code>tasks.md</code>. The three siblings are <strong>deliberately enriched</strong> — they inline the ORM, the framework, the file paths, so an implementer reading only that folder needs nothing else. This inverts the single-source-of-truth rule every other spec follows, and it is the point: the cost is paid once per feature, and every run afterwards reads less.<br /><br />Three writers now produce those files, each gated. <code>feature-architect</code> runs <strong>one feature at a time</strong> — it is the only stage that mints anchors, and two concurrent writers would both find no <code>### Entity: User</code> and both declare themselves its origin. And because the enrichment only pays off when a scope covers <em>one</em> feature, the driver now enforces that rather than asking the orchestrator for it: a bundled scope reads three inlined copies of the same decisions, which is worse than what it replaced, and nothing would have errored.',
    changes: [
      {
        type: 'added',
        description: '<strong>The feature folder.</strong> <code>gspec/features/&lt;slug&gt;/</code> holds <code>prd.md</code>, <code>arch.md</code>, <code>design.html</code>, and <code>tasks.md</code>. The PRD stays product-agnostic; its three siblings are not, on purpose — the agnosticism floor now draws that boundary <em>inside</em> one directory, by basename. The slug appears exactly once in the tree, so a feature with no PRD is visible in an <code>ls</code> rather than only to the cross-referencer.',
      },
      {
        type: 'added',
        description: '<strong>Anchor discipline, with a deterministic backstop.</strong> Every item in <code>arch.md</code> is an H3 in an exact grammar (<code>### Entity: Order</code>, <code>### Endpoint: POST /orders</code>) under the section that owns it, opened by a status line naming it an <strong>origin</strong> or an <strong>amendment</strong> of an earlier one. Exactly one origin per anchor exists across the project — a later feature that changes an entity writes a delta that says what changed, rather than a second definition that can silently disagree. Tasks carry an <code>arch:</code> line naming the anchors they touch, so a continuation run loads a few blocks instead of the whole file.',
      },
      {
        type: 'added',
        description: '<strong>A deterministic lint runs before every feature-folder validator.</strong> Heading grammar, section shape, duplicate anchors, two origins for one anchor, screen coverage between <code>arch.md</code> and <code>design.html</code> in both directions, and <code>arch:</code> anchors that do not resolve are all pure regex — so they go straight back to the writer with the exact violations at <strong>zero agent cost</strong>, and a precise instruction instead of a vague FAIL. The agent validators then only ever judge mechanically-clean drafts, and spend their whole budget on what regex cannot decide.',
      },
      {
        type: 'changed',
        description: '<strong>One feature per implement scope, enforced by the driver.</strong> The orchestrator decides ordering and concurrency; it no longer decides how much context an implementer carries. Any scope naming more than one feature is split into siblings in the same wave, and the split is logged. The no-plan fallback — which used to put <em>every</em> plan file into a single scope, reached by a JSON parse hiccup rather than a decision — now yields one scope per feature too. Each scope\'s prompt carries the exact resolved read list.',
      },
      {
        type: 'changed',
        description: '<strong>The plan gate is real now.</strong> <code>plan-validator</code> was non-blocking with no revision loop, and the plan stage\'s only checkpoint was "does the file exist" — so a plan that existed was never re-validated even after a QA failure. All three per-feature stages now gate properly, with a per-feature <code>written</code>/<code>passed</code> record that tells "already written, re-validate" apart from "the writer died here". <strong>A build that used to march past a bad plan now pauses.</strong>',
      },
      {
        type: 'changed',
        description: '<strong>The style guide is tokens only.</strong> Color, typography, spacing, elevation, radius, themes, icons, accessibility — the vector for implementation, never the concrete elements. Component styling, imagery direction, and usage examples move to each feature\'s <code>design.html</code>, which copies the token block in and references it with <code>var(--…)</code>. <code>style.html</code> is now a real recommendation rather than a preference: only the HTML form gives a design a custom-property block to copy.',
      },
      {
        type: 'changed',
        description: '<strong>Writers write their own files.</strong> <code>plan-decomposer</code> was the only writer in the roster that did not — the driver marshalled a plan out of its stdout, a leftover from an approval gate that lived in the command. It, <code>feature-architect</code>, and <code>feature-designer</code> now all write directly, which means all three get the delivery-check machinery (retry on a writer that exits clean having written nothing; accept an artifact left behind by a post-write crash) that the plan stage never had.',
      },
      {
        type: 'fixed',
        description: '<strong>The task-immutability block would have gone silent.</strong> It is the only hard PreToolUse block on a spec path, it fails open, and its matcher knew one path shape. The instant plans moved into the feature folder it would have stopped firing — no error, no log, checked tasks quietly editable — and the Codex baseline snapshot would have gone empty, so that gate would have reported clean having compared against nothing. Both now derive from one shared predicate that covers both layouts, with a regression test naming exactly that.',
      },
    ],
    migration: 'Run <code>npx gspec</code>, then <code>/gspec-migrate</code> in each project. It moves <code>features/&lt;slug&gt;.md</code> → <code>features/&lt;slug&gt;/prd.md</code> and <code>tasks/&lt;slug&gt;.md</code> → <code>features/&lt;slug&gt;/tasks.md</code>, preserving every checked task exactly. It will <strong>not</strong> invent the <code>arch.md</code> and <code>design.html</code> your existing features do not have — that is a judgment call, not a reformat — so it reports which features lack them and leaves <code>/gspec-plan</code> to write them (your <code>tasks.md</code> survives). Both layouts stay readable throughout, so a half-migrated project still plans and builds. Your style guide is left untouched: the specimens it holds have nowhere to go until the features are re-planned, so they simply read as over-budget, which is advisory.',
  },
  {
    version: '3.1.0',
    date: 'July 28, 2026',
    title: 'The architecture stops growing',
    summary: 'One document was quietly absorbing every technical decision a project ever made. <code>architecture.md</code> held module boundaries <em>and</em> every entity\'s field list, every endpoint signature, every algorithm and resolved edge case — so it grew with each feature, and by feature thirty it was over budget and every agent paid the tax on every run. The two-tier split shipped last release helped multi-module systems for a while; it did not change the growth rate.<br /><br />This release re-cuts what the tiers <em>hold</em>. The architecture is now the <strong>stable</strong> half of the technical spec: system context, module boundaries, what each module owns, how modules talk, and where code goes. The detail that grows with every feature belongs to the feature that introduces it. The writer gets one test — <strong>"would this change if we added one more feature?"</strong> — and the validator raises a <strong>major</strong> finding, naming the content and where it belongs, for field lists, endpoint signatures, algorithms, and per-screen detail. Budgets follow: 3,000 words becomes 2,100, and the module tier gets its own 600-word ceiling — which it never had, because <code>budgetFor()</code> keyed on exact paths and returned <code>null</code> for every sub-file.<br /><br />Underneath, the floors get a shared vocabulary. Each one used to re-derive "is this a gspec spec?" with its own regex, and they disagreed: <code>gspec/architecture/&lt;name&gt;.md</code> was version-checked but <strong>never agnosticism-guarded</strong>, because it matched none of the basenames in a hard-coded list. Per-module architecture shipped a full release unguarded. One module now answers that question for every floor.',
    changes: [
      {
        type: 'changed',
        description: '<strong>The architecture is high-level, and the validator keeps it that way.</strong> System tier: overview and context, module boundaries, the shared data model <em>at the name level</em> (entities and relationships — not their shape), inter-module contracts, cross-cutting auth, environment, the Modules table, and the gap analysis. Module tier: identity and boundary, owned directories, internal structure and file-placement rules, local config — about a page, and stable. Entity field lists, endpoint signatures, algorithms, business rules, state machines, and per-screen detail are now defects here, each a major finding that names where the content belongs instead.',
      },
      {
        type: 'changed',
        description: '<strong>The layout is always two tiers; one module just carries both in one file.</strong> A single-module project keeps everything in <code>gspec/architecture.md</code> exactly as before — a second file for one module is pure ceremony. What changed is the framing: the tiers are a property of the spec, not of the file count, so the same required sections apply whether they live in one file or several.',
      },
      {
        type: 'removed',
        description: '<strong>Module sub-files no longer carry a <code>covers:</code> list.</strong> Which features touch a module changes with every feature, so maintaining that index on the module tier would have made the <em>stable</em> file the most-edited one in the repo — and by an agent that should not hold write access to it. The mapping is derived from the features instead. The three consumers that routed on <code>covers:</code> now route on module boundaries and the Modules-table links.',
      },
      {
        type: 'added',
        description: '<strong>The architecture stage checks both tiers before calling itself done.</strong> A stage\'s <code>outputs</code> answer "did the writer produce its artifact?", but the architecture\'s deliverable is a <em>set</em>: the system file plus one <code>architecture/&lt;name&gt;.md</code> per row of its own Modules table. The root existing never implied the module tier did, so a multi-module run could report success having written half its architecture — with every later stage then routing against boundaries that were never written, and nothing erroring. The required set is now derived from the table (the same authority <code>verify.sh</code> uses) and every file in it must be present.',
      },
      {
        type: 'fixed',
        description: '<strong>Per-module architecture was never agnosticism-guarded.</strong> The floor matched a hard-coded list of spec <em>basenames</em>, and <code>architecture/api.md</code> is not one of them — so product identity could leak into sub-architecture files with nothing to catch it. Both floors now share one path vocabulary (<code>paths.mjs</code>), which fixes this class of bug by construction: a spec kind is described once and every floor agrees about it. A companion <code>modules.mjs</code> parses the Modules table and reports drift in both directions — a row with no file, and a file no row claims, which is exactly what a half-finished rename leaves behind.',
      },
      {
        type: 'fixed',
        description: '<strong>The installer\'s outdated-spec check skipped sub-architectures and plans.</strong> It walked <code>gspec/</code>, <code>features/</code>, and <code>epics/</code> only, so a project could be told every spec was current while <code>architecture/*.md</code> and <code>tasks/*.md</code> still carried the previous <code>spec-version</code>. Both directories are now included.',
      },
      {
        type: 'changed',
        description: '<strong><code>/gspec-migrate</code> reports architecture altitude rather than fixing it.</strong> When it finds feature-level detail still sitting in an architecture spec, it says how much and what kind and tells you to re-run <code>/gspec-architect</code>. Splitting that content across features is a judgment call that rewrites specs you already reviewed — migration stays mechanical: renames, relocations, version stamps. No files move in this release.',
      },
    ],
    migration: 'Run <code>npx gspec</code> to install the updated agents, skills, and floors. Existing architecture specs keep working and keep their layout — nothing moves. To get the new shape, re-run <code>/gspec-architect</code>: it will thin the system tier and write the module tier. Until you do, expect an advisory over-budget note on <code>architecture.md</code> (2,100 words, down from 3,000) and, on your next <code>/gspec-audit</code> or architecture QA pass, major findings naming feature-level detail that now belongs to the feature that introduced it.',
  },
  {
    version: '3.0.0',
    date: 'July 28, 2026',
    title: 'Deployables become modules, and the spec version has one home',
    summary: 'The first of four releases re-cutting how gspec organizes a specification. This one is deliberately mechanical — a rename and a version bump, no behavior changes — because it touches the same files the layout work ahead touches, and doing it second would mean editing everything twice.<br /><br /><strong>"Deployable" was always the wrong word.</strong> The table\'s own definition has been "independently build/test-able unit" since it shipped — deployment was never the criterion, and a shared library row builds and tests while deploying nowhere. The <strong>Deployables & Verification</strong> section is now <strong>Modules & Verification</strong>, sub-file frontmatter carries <code>module:</code> instead of <code>deployable:</code>, and <code>verify.sh</code> prints <code>FAIL: &lt;module&gt;:&lt;phase&gt;</code>. Because that key is the row <em>name</em>, which does not change, <strong>existing <code>verify.sh</code> scripts keep working untouched.</strong><br /><br />The release also fixes a quiet hazard: <code>SPEC_VERSION</code> existed in <strong>four</strong> places with nothing asserting they agreed — the installer, the emit script, the spec-integrity floor, and the prose in the harness preamble. A drift between any two would have meant specs marked current by one check and stale by another. There is now one exported constant, two importers, and a test that fails if the two copies that <em>cannot</em> import (the floor ships standalone into <code>.claude/hooks/floors/</code>; the preamble is prose) fall out of step.',
    changes: [
      {
        type: 'changed',
        description: '<strong>"Deployable" is now "module" everywhere.</strong> The <strong>Deployables & Verification</strong> section becomes <strong>Modules & Verification</strong>; per-unit architecture sub-files carry <code>module:</code> frontmatter; <code>verify.sh</code> fail-fast lines read <code>FAIL: &lt;module&gt;:&lt;build|test&gt;</code>; and the audit\'s <code>deployable</code> drift category becomes <code>module</code>. The rename is about accuracy, not taste — the criterion was always "independently build/test-able", never "deployed". The table itself, its <code>name · dir · build · test</code> columns, the one-row/many-row layout gate, and the two-tier file layout are all unchanged.',
